{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 804bcd1f9af3cc050733688d3f7186e466aacbb6, artifact c434cdd076e494426ff039b083809491089283a0.\n\nThe number Hugh saw was 413, and it is worth stating what that was, because the shape of the mistake is more interesting than the arithmetic. It was 223 commitments carrying a stale or disputed qualifier, plus 190 retired-or-stale artifacts, added together and rendered in the header chip as \"+413 need attention\". `gs status` reports those two populations on separate lines and never adds them, so the browser's figure corresponded to nothing on either surface — and the natural reading, that one of the two must be miscounting, was false.\n\nUnderneath the mixing was a second fault that would have kept the numbers wrong even without it. Attention was treated as a third slice beside Active and Closed, when staleness and dispute are qualifiers sitting on top of a lifecycle status. Measured on the live log, 188 of those 223 commitments are already counted as active or closed, so the three figures summed past the number of commitments that exist. A third, smaller inconsistency sat beside it: Active was read from the projection while Attention and Closed were summed over topics, so a commitment whose root is not a topic was inside one number and outside the others.\n\nThe resolution the earlier request left open is answered by the arithmetic rather than by preference, and I want to be explicit that this was luck rather than judgement. That request framed it as a choice — partition like the CLI and lose the artifact signal, or keep the mixed count and label it honestly. Status turns out to partition cleanly: 75 active, 411 closed and 35 lifecycle-stale sum to exactly the 521 commitments in the log. So the three headline numbers can be checkable against the CLI *and* the artifact signal can stay, reported beside them. Had the arithmetic not allowed both, I would have had to choose, and that would have been a product decision to bring back rather than make.\n\nWhat changed: the three counts now come from one source and one rule, `workCommitmentCounts`. Attention is labelled as the qualifier it is, saying in the description that it overlaps the other two. Artifacts needing attention are reported separately, and the header chip's tooltip now names both figures instead of one sum. Both call sites went through `workAttentionCount`, so the fix is at the root rather than patched twice.\n\nOne thing a reviewer should look at directly rather than take from me: I changed an existing test. It asserted `workAttentionCount === 3` for one stale commitment, one stale artifact and one unlinked promise — it encoded the mixing as correct, so it had to change with the behaviour. It now asserts 1 and 2 separately with the reason written in the test. I would rather that be named here than found in a commit that also changes what the test was measuring.\n\nAlso worth saying plainly: I fixed this before filing the request, because Hugh asked me to find and fix it directly. The durable request should have come first. I filed it from the evidence afterwards rather than pretending the order was right.\n\nGates: `npx tsc --noEmit` clean, UI tests pass 71 of 71. The worktree is clean at the reported head, cut from current main at 97ea67a1. I have not run the Go suite; nothing in this change touches Go, which is an inference the reviewer should weigh as one.","body":{"head":"804bcd1f9af3cc050733688d3f7186e466aacbb6","status":"ready-for-review"}}